window.docContents[162].resolve({"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/Earlier-approaches/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Earlier-approaches":{"contents":"There are two established ways to obtain this guarantee. An external algebra\nsystem can propose an answer that a proof assistant checks. Lewis and Wu's\nLean–Mathematica interface follows this approach: it translates expressions\nbetween the systems and reconstructs proofs for selected returned computations\n. The search can be powerful without making\nthe external calculator part of the proof's trusted basis.\n\nAlternatively, an algorithm can be defined inside the proof assistant and\nproved correct once. Computational reflection evaluates that algorithm on an\nexpression tree and applies its correctness theorem; Boutin developed this\napproach for certified decision procedures in Coq\n. PDEAtlas uses this pattern for its supported\nsymbolic transformations and interval evaluators. The\nRelated Work chapter compares the surrounding projects.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Verified Symbolic and Continuous Computation","header":"10.1. Earlier approaches","id":"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/Earlier-approaches/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Earlier-approaches"}});